    <node id="吴国">
      <data key="d0">吴国</data>
      <data key="d1">地点</data>
      <data key="d2">吴国, also known as the State of Wu, was an ancient Chinese state that played a significant role in the historical conflicts of the region. It is primarily recognized as an adversary of the State of Yue, particularly during the era of its notable ruler, King Fu Chai (吴王夫差). The State of Wu is often associated with its enmity towards the Yue, especially in the context of the legendary figure Gou Jian (勾践), who was a prominent enemy of Wu.

Geographically, Wu was situated in the eastern part of ancient China, which contributed to its strategic importance and military engagements. The state is also linked to the legendary swordsmiths Gan Jiang and Mo Ye, who are said to have originated from Wu, further embedding the state in Chinese cultural narratives.

Additionally, Wu was notable for its political alliances and rivalries, including the involvement of key figures such as Fan Li (范蠡) and Wen Zhong (文种), who were known to have traveled to Wu. This highlights the state's role not only as a military power but also as a center of political activity and intrigue during its time. Overall, Wu's legacy is marked by its conflicts, cultural contributions, and its historical significance in the tapestry of ancient Chinese states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6f4631a6bc7286dd9560f06b9845d9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a72fd2ce03a23a58c65d98d2258f3e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89960ee186e8b69358a7a5c384a3ee9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3e590b3ab7f94ee3770d3739196401&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939cc8f0980bb7b81255939246f7fe66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76f36e0abe52b904e30fbb2a78b9a4a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2a680eed881fd86d938031d895d21e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f20b87683fe0cc8608a321ff18feb71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e93cef7b7f368309937f9f274b9ad084</data>
